# The Hidden Cost of a Slow Hire in Telecom – TalentPartners

[Skip to content](#content)

# The Hidden Cost of a Slow Hire in Telecom

The telecom industry is in the middle of a massive buildout wave—driven by fiber expansion, BEAD funding, and rural broadband initiatives. In this environment, a slow hire isn't just an HR problem. It's a project risk.

## Why Hiring Speed Matters More in Telecom Than Almost Any Other Industry

**Projects stall without people.**
Fiber deployment requires a coordinated sequence of steps: feasibility, design, permitting, make-ready, and construction. Each of those steps requires specific expertise—OSP engineers, GIS analysts, designers, project managers, and construction managers. If any key role goes unfilled, the project timeline slips.

**The labor market is tight.**
Demand for telecom talent is significantly outpacing supply. BEAD alone is expected to create tens of thousands of new jobs. But experienced OSP engineers, fiber designers, and GIS analysts don't appear overnight. Many have 10-15 years of experience that simply can't be fast-tracked.

**The cost compounds quickly.**
A 30-day delay in hiring an OSP Engineer could mean:
- A delayed permit submission, setting the project back weeks or months
- A missed construction window
- Liquidated damages if you're under contract

**Recruitment timelines are getting longer.**
Even in a strong candidate market, specialized telecom roles take 30-60 days to fill on average. Without an active pipeline, you're already behind.

## What to Do About It

**Build your pipeline before you need it.** Don't wait until a project is awarded to start recruiting. The best candidates are usually already employed and need time to transition.

**Partner with specialists.** Generic staffing firms don't understand what an "OSP Designer proficient in Katapult and Biarri" means. We do.

**Move fast when you find the right candidate.** Extended interview processes lose top candidates to competitors.

## The Bottom Line

In telecom, time is money. A slow hire creates a ripple effect that hits your project schedule, your client relationships, and your bottom line. Speed and quality aren't mutually exclusive—but they require a partner who understands both.

